  "response": "Zoologists discriminate between rival explanations of a mass mortality event by deriving from each a prediction that the other does not make, and then testing those predictions against the quantitative detail of the field record. The saiga antelope collapse in Kazakhstan is the standard worked example of the procedure. Roughly 200,000 saiga antelope died in central Kazakhstan in May 2015. The die-off coincided with the saiga calving aggregation, and that concentration of the population into dense maternal groups drove mortality within the affected herds to approach 100 percent, a level which the sheer scale of the recorded losses confirms. In other words, almost no animals in the affected herds survived.\n\nThe first discriminating test concerns the physical environment, since an infectious explanation and an environmental-toxin explanation assign different weight to antecedent weather. Investigators compared meteorological records with the precise timing of the deaths. On a separate point, the 2015 die-off occurred in January during subzero temperatures. At least one of the following held in the week before the deaths, and perhaps both: ambient temperatures in that week exceeded the long-term seasonal average, and humidity in that week exceeded the long-term seasonal average.\n\nThe second test exploits dose dependence, which separates poisoning from infection. A plant-toxicosis explanation predicts mortality graded by the quantity of plant material ingested, and that prediction cannot be squared with the near-total loss already described, because uniform death across every age and condition class leaves behind no gradient of exposure. That same prediction led investigators to examine the stomach and rumen contents of dead animals for ingested plant species. The decisive laboratory result, by contrast, emerged not from the gut but from the tissues. Because Pasteurella multocida type B was isolated from tissue samples taken from dead saiga, the microbiology establishes what the international expert review panel concluded, namely that haemorrhagic septicaemia caused by Pasteurella multocida type B produced the die-off.\n\nA third line of evidence is geometric, because transmission from animal to animal leaves a clustered mortality footprint, whereas a shared environmental hazard instead marks out the extent of the hazard itself. Carcass locations were mapped to test for spatial clustering of mortality. On a separate matter, Pasteurella multocida type B spreads between saiga through tick bites. Across these tests the logic is cumulative: each explanation is pressed for the single observation it alone requires, and the account that survives every such confrontation is the account of the deaths.",
